Introduction
20.1
Banks can choose between two broad methodologies for calculating their risk-based capital
requirements for credit risk. The first is the standardised approach, which is set out in chapters
CRE20 to CRE22:
The standardised approach assigns standardised risk weights to exposures as described
1.
in this chapter, CRE20. Risk weighted assets are calculated as the product of the
standardised risk weights and the exposure amount. Exposures should be risk-weighted
net of specific provisions (including partial write-oﬀs).
To determine the risk weights in the standardised approach for certain exposure classes,
2.
in jurisdictions that allow the use of external ratings for regulatory purposes, banks may,
as a starting point, use assessments by external credit assessment institutions that are
recognised as eligible for capital purposes by national supervisors. The requirements
covering the use of external ratings are set out in chapter CRE21.
84
The credit risk mitigation techniques that are permitted to be recognised under the
3.
standardised approach are set out in chapter CRE22.
1 Footnote
84
The notations in CRE20 to CRE22 follow the methodology used by one
institution, Standard and Poor’s (S&P). The use of S&P credit ratings is an
example only; those of some other external credit assessment institutions could
equally well be used. The ratings used throughout this document, therefore, do
not express any preferences or determinations on external assessment
institutions by the Committee.
20.2
The second risk-weighted capital treatment for measuring credit risk, the internal ratings-based
(IRB) approach, allows banks to use their internal rating systems for credit risk, subject to the
explicit approval of the bank’s supervisor. The IRB approach is set out in CRE30 to CRE36.","Basel Framework, CRE20, num. 20.1-20.2, pp. 179-180","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split","0.99","false",""
