Exposures to banks
20.16
For the purposes of calculating capital requirements, a bank exposure is defined as a claim
(including loans and senior debt instruments, unless considered as subordinated debt for the
purposes of CRE20.60) on any financial institution that is licensed to take deposits from the
public and is subject to appropriate prudential standards and level of supervision.
93 The
treatment associated with subordinated bank debt and equities is addressed in CRE20.53 to
CRE20.60.
1 Footnote
93
For internationally active banks, appropriate prudential standards (eg capital
and liquidity requirements) and level of supervision should be in accordance
with the Basel framework. For domestic banks, appropriate prudential
standards are determined by the national supervisors but should include at
least a minimum regulatory capital requirement.
