The following collateral instruments are eligible for recognition in the simple approach:
Cash (as well as certificates of deposit or comparable instruments issued by the lending
1.
bank) on deposit with the bank that is incurring the counterparty exposure.
136
137
Gold.
2.
In jurisdictions that allow the use of external ratings for regulatory purposes:
3.
Debt securities rated
138 by a recognised external credit assessment institution
1.
(ECAI) where these are either:
(i)
at least BB– when issued by sovereigns or public sector entities (PSEs) that are
treated as sovereigns by the national supervisor; or
(ii)
at least BBB– when issued by other entities (including banks and other prudentially
regulated financial institutions); or
(iii)
at least A-3/P-3 for short-term debt instruments.
Debt securities not rated by a recognised ECAI where these are:
2.
(i)
issued by a bank; and
(ii)
listed on a recognised exchange; and
(iii)
classified as senior debt; and
(iv)
all rated issues of the same seniority by the issuing bank are rated at least BBB– or
A-3/P-3 by a recognised ECAI; and
(v)
the bank holding the securities as collateral has no information to suggest that the
issue justifies a rating below BBB– or A-3/P-3 (as applicable); and
(vi)
the supervisor is suﬃciently confident that the market liquidity of the security is
adequate.
In jurisdictions that do not allow the use of external ratings for regulatory purposes, the
4.
following securities will be eligible provided that the supervisor is suﬃciently confident
that the market liquidity of the security is adequate:
Debt securities issued by sovereigns or PSEs that are treated as sovereigns by the
1.
national supervisor;
Debt securities issued by banks assigned to Grade A under the standardised credit
2.
risk assessment approach;
Other debt securities issued by “investment grade” entities as defined in
3.
CRE22.76, and
Securitisation exposures with a risk weight of less than 100% in the Securitisation
4.
Standardised Approach set out in CRE41.
Equities (including convertible bonds) that are included in a main index.
5.
Undertakings for Collective Investments in Transferable Securities (UCITS) and mutual
6.
funds where:
a price for the units is publicly quoted daily; and
1.
the UCITS/mutual fund is limited to investing in the instruments listed in this
2.
paragraph.
139
4 Footnotes
136
Cash-funded credit-linked notes issued by the bank against exposures in the
banking book that fulfil the criteria for credit derivatives are treated as cash-
collateralised transactions.
