A guarantee (counter-guarantee) or credit derivative must satisfy the following requirements:
it represents a direct claim on the protection provider;
1.
it is explicitly referenced to specific exposures or a pool of exposures, so that the extent
2.
of the cover is clearly defined and incontrovertible;
other than non-payment by a protection purchaser of money due in respect of the credit
3.
protection contract it is irrevocable;
there is no clause in the contract that would allow the protection provider unilaterally to
4.
cancel the credit cover, change the maturity agreed ex post, or that would increase the
eﬀective cost of cover as a result of deteriorating credit quality in the hedged exposure;
it must be unconditional; there should be no clause in the protection contract outside the
5.
direct control of the bank that could prevent the protection provider from being obliged
to pay out in a timely manner in the event that the underlying counterparty fails to make
the payment(s) due.
22.72
In the case of maturity mismatches, the amount of credit protection that is provided must be
