In addition to the legal certainty requirements in CRE22.9, in order for a guarantee to be
recognised, the following requirements must be satisfied:
On the qualifying default/non-payment of the counterparty, the bank may in a timely
1.
manner pursue the guarantor for any monies outstanding under the documentation
governing the transaction. The guarantor may make one lump sum payment of all
monies under such documentation to the bank, or the guarantor may assume the future
payment obligations of the counterparty covered by the guarantee. The bank must have
the right to receive any such payments from the guarantor without first having to take
legal action in order to pursue the counterparty for payment.
The guarantee is an explicitly documented obligation assumed by the guarantor.
2.
Except as noted in the following sentence, the guarantee covers all types of payments
3.
the underlying counterparty is expected to make under the documentation governing the
transaction, for example notional amount, margin payments, etc. Where a guarantee
covers payment of principal only, interests and other uncovered payments must be
treated as an unsecured amount in accordance with the rules for proportional cover
described in CRE22.80.
