In addition to the transactions considered in CRE32.51 above, other short-term exposures with
an original maturity of less than one year that are not part of a bank’s ongoing financing of an
obligor may be eligible for exemption from the one-year floor. After a careful review of the
particular circumstances in their jurisdictions, national supervisors should define the types of
short-term exposures that might be considered eligible for this treatment. The results of these
reviews might, for example, include transactions such as:
Some capital market-driven transactions and repo-style transactions that might not fall
1.
within the scope of CRE32.51.
Some trade finance transactions that are not exempted by CRE32.52.
2.
Some exposures arising from settling securities purchases and sales. This could also
3.
