As indicated in CRE33.3, each supervisory category broadly corresponds to a range of external
credit assessments.
33.7
At national discretion, supervisors may allow banks to assign preferential risk weights of 70%
to “strong” exposures, and 95% to “good” exposures, provided they have a remaining maturity
of less than 2.5 years or the supervisor determines that banks’ underwriting and other risk
characteristics are substantially stronger than specified in the slotting criteria for the relevant
supervisory risk category.
