"c_538e09bcd566583c","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026","Basel Framework","Basel III - Basel Framework consolidado","BCBS/BIS","INT","international_standard","in_force","","2026-01-01","","https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/basel-framework.pdf","fuentes_descargadas/02_normativa_internacional/18_bis_basel_framework_2026.pdf","acf3759fc231fb8f13131e50b12ad11e18bc9ebe53f105595fd8e16609a3547f","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026#nsec-10-occ4","10","numbered_section","OPE Calculation of RWA for operational risk > 10 Definitions and application > num. 10.1","763","764","OPE10
Definitions and application
This chapter defines operational risk and the
components of the Business Indicator used to
calculate capital requirements for operational
risk. In addition, this chapter describes the
application within a banking group of the
standardised approach for measuring
operational risk capital requirements.
Version eﬀective as of 01
January 2023
Reflects revised standardised approach
introduced in the December 2017 Basel III
publication (including the revised
implementation date announced on 27 March
2020) and removal of internal model
approaches. Updated to incorporate the FAQ
published on 30 March 2023 and the FAQ
published on 5 July 2024.
Definition of operational risk
10.1
Operational risk is defined as the risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal
processes, people and systems or from external events. This definition includes legal risk,
361
but excludes strategic and reputational risk.
1 Footnote
361
Legal risk includes, but is not limited to, exposure to fines, penalties, or punitive
damages resulting from supervisory actions, as well as private settlements.
Definition of Business Indicator components","Basel Framework, OPE10, num. 10.1, pp. 763-764","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split","0.99","false",""
