All of the following principles must be satisfied in order for a jurisdiction to qualify for
alternative treatment.
To use the alternative treatment under the LCR, a jurisdiction must demonstrate and
1.
justify that insuﬃcient HQLA denominated in the domestic currency exists, taking into
account all relevant factors aﬀecting the supply of, and demand for, such HQLA (Principle
1).
The supply of HQLA in the domestic currency of the jurisdiction must be
1.
insuﬃcient, in terms of Level 1 assets only or both Level 1 and Level 2 assets, to
meet the aggregate demand for such assets from banks operating in that currency.
The jurisdiction must be able to provide adequate information (quantitative and
otherwise) to demonstrate this aggregate shortfall.
The determination of insuﬃcient HQLA by the jurisdiction under LCR31.20(1)(a)
2.
should address all major factors relevant to the issue. These include, but are not
limited to, the expected supply of HQLA in the medium term (eg three to five
years), the extent to which the banking sector can and should run less liquidity risk,
and the competing demand from banks and non-bank investors for holding HQLA
for similar or other purposes.
Insuﬃcient HQLA faced by the jurisdiction must be caused by structural, policy and
3.
other constraints that cannot be resolved within the medium term (eg three to five
years). Such constraints may relate to the fiscal or budget policies of the
jurisdiction, the infrastructural development of its capital markets, the structure of
its monetary system and operations (eg the currency board arrangements for
jurisdictions with pegged exchange rates), or other jurisdiction-specific factors
leading to the shortage or imbalance in the supply of HQLA available to the
banking sector.
A jurisdiction that intends to adopt one or more of the options for alternative treatment
2.
must be capable of limiting the uncertainty of performance, or mitigating the risks of
non-performance, of the option(s) concerned (Principle 2).
For Option 1 (ie the provision of contractual committed liquidity facilities from the
1.
relevant central bank for a fee), the jurisdiction must have the economic strength
to support the committed liquidity facilities granted by its central bank. To ensure
this, the jurisdiction should have a process in place to control the aggregate
amount of such facilities to within a level that can be measured and managed.
For Option 2 (ie use of foreign currency HQLA to cover domestic currency liquidity
2.
needs), the jurisdiction must have a mechanism in place to control the foreign
exchange risk of its banks’ foreign currency HQLA holdings.
For Option 3 (ie use of Level 2A assets beyond the 40% cap with a higher haircut),
3.
the jurisdiction must only allow Level 2 assets that are of a quality (credit and
liquidity) comparable to that for Level 1 assets in its currency to be used under this
option. The jurisdiction should be able to provide quantitative and qualitative
evidence to substantiate this requirement.
A jurisdiction that intends to adopt one or more of the options for alternative treatment
3.
must be committed to observing all of the obligations set out below (Principle 3).
The jurisdiction must maintain a supervisory monitoring system to ensure that its
1.
banks comply with the rules and requirements relevant to their usage of the
options, including any associated haircuts, limits or restrictions.
The jurisdiction must document and update its approach to adopting an alternative
2.
treatment, and make the approach explicit and transparent to other national
supervisors. The approach should address how it complies with the applicable
criteria, limits and obligations set out in the qualifying principles, including the
determination of insuﬃcient HQLA and other key aspects of its framework for
alternative treatment.
The jurisdiction must review periodically the determination of insuﬃcient HQLA at
3.
intervals not exceeding five years, and disclose the results of review and any
consequential changes to other national supervisors and stakeholders.
The jurisdiction must permit an independent peer review of its framework for
4.
alternative treatment to be conducted as part of the Basel Committee’s work
programme and address the comments made.","Basel Framework, LCR31, num. 31.20, pp. 849-850","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split","0.99","false",""
