Where the self-assessment reflects that insuﬃcient HQLA no longer exists, the jurisdiction
should devise a plan for transition to the standard HQLA treatment under the LCR and notify
the Basel Committee accordingly. If the issue of insuﬃciency remains but weaknesses in the
jurisdiction’s relevant supervisory framework are identified from the self-assessment, the
jurisdiction should disclose its plan to address those weaknesses within a reasonable period.
31.61
If the jurisdiction is aware of circumstances (eg relating to fiscal conditions, market
infrastructure or availability of liquidity, etc.) that have radically changed to an extent that may
render insuﬃcient HQLA no longer relevant to the jurisdiction, it must conduct a self-
assessment promptly (ie without waiting until the next self-assessment is due) and notify the
Basel Committee of the result as soon as practicable. The Basel Committee may similarly
request the jurisdiction to conduct a self-assessment ahead of schedule if the Basel Committee
is aware of changes that will significantly aﬀect the jurisdiction’s eligibility for alternative
