Principle 3: Laws, regulations or other arrangements provide a framework for cooperation and
collaboration with relevant domestic authorities and foreign supervisors. These arrangements
reflect the need to protect confidential information.
621
1 Footnote
621
Principle 3 is developed further in Principle 12 BCP40.27, Principle 13 BCP40.30
and Principle 29 BCP40.66.","Basel Framework, BCP40, num. 40.8, p. 1367","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split+page_split","0.99","false",""
"c_a05d5bbc03aaa6e4","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026","Basel Framework","Basel III - Basel Framework consolidado","BCBS/BIS","INT","international_standard","in_force","","2026-01-01","","https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/basel-framework.pdf","fuentes_descargadas/02_normativa_internacional/18_bis_basel_framework_2026.pdf","acf3759fc231fb8f13131e50b12ad11e18bc9ebe53f105595fd8e16609a3547f","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026#nsec-40-occ9","40","numbered_section","BCP Core Principles for effective banking supervision > 40 The Core Principles and assessment criteria > num. 40.9","1367","1368","40.9
Essential criteria:
Arrangements, whether formal or informal, are in place for cooperation, including
1.
analysis and sharing of information and undertaking collaborative work, with all domestic
authorities with responsibility for the safety and soundness of banks, other financial
institutions and/or the stability of the financial system. There is evidence that these
arrangements work in practice, where necessary.
Arrangements, whether formal or informal, are in place for the supervisor to coordinate,
2.
within its mandate, with relevant authorities with responsibility for macroprudential
policy when undertaking actions related to monitoring, identifying and addressing
systemic risks that have the potential to aﬀect the stability of the banking system.
Arrangements, whether formal or informal, are in place for cooperation, including
3.
analysis and sharing of information and undertaking collaborative work, with relevant
foreign supervisors of banks. There is evidence that these arrangements work in practice,
where necessary.
The supervisor may provide confidential information to another domestic authority or
4.
foreign supervisor but must take reasonable steps to determine that any confidential
information so released will be used only for bank-specific or system-wide supervisory
purposes and will be treated as confidential by the receiving party.
The supervisor receiving confidential information from other supervisors uses the
5.
confidential information for bank-specific or system-wide supervisory purposes only. The
supervisor does not disclose to third parties confidential information received without the
permission of the supervisor providing the information and is able to deny any demand
(other than a court order or mandate from a legislative body) to disclose confidential
information in its possession. If the supervisor is legally compelled to disclose confidential
information it has received from another supervisor, it promptly notifies the originating
supervisor, indicating what information it is compelled to release and the circumstances
surrounding the release. Where consent to passing on confidential information is not
given, the supervisor uses all reasonable means to resist such a demand or protect the
confidentiality of the information.
Processes are in place for the supervisor to support resolution authorities (eg central
6.
banks and finance ministries as appropriate) undertaking recovery and resolution
planning and actions.
Principle 4 - Permissible activities","Basel Framework, BCP40, num. 40.9, pp. 1367-1368","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split+page_split","0.99","false",""
"c_1afc8802c191cd12","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026","Basel Framework","Basel III - Basel Framework consolidado","BCBS/BIS","INT","international_standard","in_force","","2026-01-01","","https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/basel-framework.pdf","fuentes_descargadas/02_normativa_internacional/18_bis_basel_framework_2026.pdf","acf3759fc231fb8f13131e50b12ad11e18bc9ebe53f105595fd8e16609a3547f","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026#nsec-40-occ9","40","numbered_section","BCP Core Principles for effective banking supervision > 40 The Core Principles and assessment criteria > num. 40.10-40.12","1368","1369","40.10
Principle 4: The permissible activities of institutions that are licensed and subject to supervision
as banks are clearly defined, and the use of the word ""bank"" in names is controlled.
40.11
Essential criteria:
The term ""bank"" is clearly defined in laws or regulations.
1.
The permissible activities of institutions that are licensed and subject to supervision as
2.
banks are clearly defined either by supervisors, or in laws or regulations.
The use of the word ""bank"" and any derivations, such as ""banking"", in a name, including
3.
domain names, is limited to licensed and supervised institutions in all circumstances
where the general public might otherwise be misled.
The taking of deposits from the public is reserved for institutions that are licensed and
4.
subject to supervision as banks.
622
The supervisor or licensing authority publishes or otherwise makes available a current list
5.
of licensed banks, including branches of foreign banks, operating within its jurisdiction in
a way that is easily accessible to the public.
1 Footnote
622
The Committee recognises the existence of non-bank financial institutions that
take deposits but may be regulated diﬀerently from banks. These institutions
should be subject to a form of regulation commensurate to the type and size of
their business and, collectively, should not hold a significant proportion of
deposits in the financial system.
Principle 5 - Licensing criteria
40.12
Principle 5:
623 The licensing authority has the power to set criteria for licensing banks and to
reject applications where the criteria are not met. At a minimum, the licensing process consists
of an assessment of the ownership structure and governance (including the fitness and
propriety of board members and senior management) of the bank and its wider group, its
strategic and operating plan, internal controls, risk management and projected financial
condition (including capital base). Where the proposed owner or parent organisation is a
foreign bank, the prior consent of its home supervisor is obtained.
1 Footnote
623
