Essential criteria:
Laws or regulations clearly define:
1.
what types and amounts (absolute and/or in relation to a bank's capital) of
1.
acquisitions and investments need prior supervisory approval; and
cases for which notification after the acquisition or investment is suﬃcient. Such
2.
cases are primarily activities closely related to banking and where the investment
is small relative to the bank's capital.
Laws or regulations provide criteria by which to judge individual bank proposals for
2.
acquisitions and investments.
The supervisor determines that any new acquisitions and investments will not expose the
3.
bank to undue risks or hinder eﬀective supervision, and (where appropriate) that they
will not hinder eﬀective implementation of corrective measures in the future.
629 The
supervisor can prohibit banks from making major acquisitions/investments (including the
establishment of cross-border banking operations) in countries with laws or regulations
prohibiting information flows deemed necessary for adequate consolidated supervision.
In making this assessment, the supervisor considers the eﬀectiveness of supervision in
the host country and its own ability to exercise supervision on a consolidated basis.
The supervisor determines that the bank has, from the outset, adequate financial,
4.
managerial and organisational resources to manage the acquisition/investment.
The supervisor is aware of the risks that non-banking activities can pose to a bank and
5.
has the means to take action to mitigate those risks. The supervisor considers the ability
of the bank to manage these risks prior to permitting investment in non-banking
activities.
The supervisor reviews major acquisitions or investments by other entities in the banking
6.
group to determine that these do not expose the bank to any undue risks or hinder
eﬀective supervision. The supervisor also determines, where appropriate, that these new
acquisitions and investments will not hinder eﬀective implementation of corrective
measures in the future. Where necessary, the supervisor is able to eﬀectively address
the risks to the bank arising from such acquisitions or investments.
1 Footnote
629
The supervisor may consider whether the acquisition or investment creates
obstacles to the orderly resolution of the bank.
