"c_506221900b4511ca","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026","Basel Framework","Basel III - Basel Framework consolidado","BCBS/BIS","INT","international_standard","in_force","","2026-01-01","","https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/basel-framework.pdf","fuentes_descargadas/02_normativa_internacional/18_bis_basel_framework_2026.pdf","acf3759fc231fb8f13131e50b12ad11e18bc9ebe53f105595fd8e16609a3547f","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026#nsec-40-occ9","40","numbered_section","BCP Core Principles for effective banking supervision > 40 The Core Principles and assessment criteria > num. 40.21","1374","1376","40.21
Essential criteria:
The supervisor employs an appropriate mix of on-site and oﬀ-site supervision to evaluate
1.
the condition of banks, their risk profile, their internal control environment and the
corrective measures necessary to address supervisory concerns. The specific mix
between on-site and oﬀ-site supervision may be determined by the particular conditions
and circumstances of the country and the bank. The supervisor regularly assesses the
quality, eﬀectiveness and integration of its on-site and oﬀ-site functions and amends its
approach, as needed.
The supervisor has a coherent process for planning and executing on-site and oﬀ-site
2.
activities. There are policies and processes to ensure that such activities are conducted
on a thorough and consistent basis with clear responsibilities, objectives and outputs,
and that there is eﬀective coordination and information-sharing between the on-site and
oﬀ-site functions.
The supervisor uses a range of information to regularly review and assess the safety and
3.
soundness of banks and the stability of the banking system, the evaluation of material
risks, and the identification of necessary corrective and supervisory actions. This includes
information such as prudential reports, statistical returns, information on a bank's related
entities and publicly available information. The information received on banks is used by
supervisors to form a holistic view and understanding of their risk profile. The supervisor
determines that information provided by banks is reliable
634 and obtains, as necessary,
additional information on banks and their related entities.
The supervisor uses a variety of tools to regularly review and assess the safety and
4.
soundness of banks and the stability of the banking system, including:The supervisor
uses its analysis to determine follow-up work required, if any.
analysis of financial statements and accounts;
1.
business model analysis;
2.
horizontal peer reviews;
3.
analysis of corporate governance, including risk management and internal control
4.
systems;
reviews of the outcome of stress tests undertaken by the banks; and
5.
assessments of the adequacy of banks' capital and liquidity levels under adverse
6.
economic scenarios, which may include conducting supervisory stress tests on
individual banks or on a system-wide basis.
Based on the information provided by banks and its own analysis, the supervisor
5.
communicates its findings to banks as appropriate and requires them to take action to
mitigate any particular vulnerabilities that have the potential to aﬀect their safety and
soundness or the stability of the banking system (including implications for and
interlinkages with financial system stability).
The supervisor evaluates the work of the bank's internal audit function (including those
6.
that are outsourced or co-sourced) and determines whether, and to what extent, it may
rely on the internal auditors' work to identify areas of potential risk.
The supervisor engages suﬃciently frequently with the bank's board, non-executive
7.
board members and senior and middle management (including heads of individual
business units and control functions) to develop an understanding of and assess matters
such as strategy, group structure, corporate governance, performance, capital adequacy,
liquidity, asset quality, risk management systems and internal controls. Where
necessary, the supervisor challenges the bank's board and senior management on the
assumptions made in setting strategies and business models.
The supervisor communicates to the bank the findings of its on- and oﬀ-site supervisory
8.
analyses in a timely manner by means of written reports or through discussions or
meetings with the bank's management. The supervisor meets with the bank's senior
management and the board to discuss the results of supervisory examinations and
external audits, as appropriate. The supervisor also meets separately with the bank's
independent board members and external auditor, as necessary.
The supervisor undertakes appropriate and timely follow-up activities to check that banks
9.
have addressed supervisory concerns or implemented requirements communicated to
them. This includes early escalation to the appropriate level of the supervisory authority
and to the bank's board if action points are not addressed in an adequate or timely
manner.
The supervisor requires banks to notify it in advance of any substantive changes in their
10.
activities, structure and overall condition, or as soon as they become aware of any
material adverse developments, including breaches of legal or prudential requirements.
The supervisor may use independent third parties, including external experts, but it
11.
cannot outsource its prudential responsibilities to third parties. Where third parties are
used, the supervisor:
clearly defines and documents their roles and responsibilities, including the scope
1.
of work where they are appointed to conduct supervisory tasks;
assesses their suitability for the designated task(s), the quality of their work and
2.
whether their output can be relied upon to the degree intended;
ensures that they are subject to appropriate confidentiality restrictions;
3.
considers the biases that may influence them; and
4.
requires that they promptly bring to its attention any material shortcomings
5.
identified during the course of any work undertaken by them for supervisory
purposes.
The supervisor has an adequate information system which facilitates the processing,
12.
monitoring and analysis of prudential information. The system aids the identification of
areas requiring follow-up action.
1 Footnote
