Essential criteria:
The supervisor understands the overall structure of the banking group and is familiar
1.
with all the material activities (including non-banking activities) conducted by entities in
the wider group, whether domestic or cross-border. The supervisor understands and
assesses how group-wide risks are managed and takes action when risks arising from the
banking group and other entities in the wider group, in particular contagion and
reputational risks, may jeopardise the safety and soundness of the bank and the banking
system.
The supervisor imposes prudential standards and collects and analyses financial and
2.
other information on a consolidated basis for the banking group, covering areas such as
capital adequacy, liquidity, large exposures, exposures to related parties, lending limits
and group structure.
The supervisor reviews whether the oversight of a bank's foreign operations by
3.
management (of the parent bank or head oﬃce and, where relevant, the holding
company) is adequate having regard to their risk profile and systemic importance. The
supervisor determines that parent banks have unimpeded access to all material
information from their foreign branches and subsidiaries. The supervisor also determines
that banks' policies and processes require the local management of any cross-border
operations to have the necessary expertise to manage those operations in a safe and
sound manner, and in compliance with supervisory and regulatory requirements. The
home supervisor considers the eﬀectiveness of supervision conducted in the host
countries in which its banks have material operations.
The home supervisor visits the foreign oﬃces of the bank periodically. The location and
4.
frequency of these visits are determined by the risk profile and systemic importance of
the bank's foreign operations. The supervisor meets the host supervisors during these
visits. The supervisor has a policy for assessing whether it needs to conduct on-site
examinations of a bank's foreign operations or require additional reporting, and it has the
power and resources to take those actions as and when appropriate.
The supervisor reviews the main activities of parent companies and of companies
5.
aﬃliated with the parent companies that have a material impact on the safety and
soundness of the bank and takes appropriate supervisory action.
The supervisor limits the range of activities the consolidated group may conduct and the
6.
locations in which activities can be conducted (including the closing of foreign oﬃces) if it
determines that:
the safety and soundness of the bank is compromised because the activities
1.
expose it to excessive risk and/or are not properly managed;
the supervision by other domestic authorities is not adequate relative to the risks
2.
the activities present; and/or
the exercise of eﬀective supervision on a consolidated basis is hindered.
3.
In addition to supervising on a consolidated basis, the responsible supervisor supervises
7.
individual banks in the group. The responsible supervisor supervises each bank on a solo
basis and understands its relationship with other members of the group.
640
