Essential criteria:
Laws or regulations establish the duties, responsibilities and powers of the supervisor
1.
related to the supervision of banks' internal controls and enforcement of compliance with
the relevant laws and regulations regarding criminal activities.
The supervisor determines that banks have adequate policies and processes that
2.
promote high ethical and professional standards and prevent the bank from being used
intentionally or unintentionally for criminal activities. This includes the monitoring,
detection and prevention of criminal activity, and reporting of such suspected activities
to the appropriate authorities.
In addition to reporting to the financial intelligence unit or other designated authorities,
3.
banks report suspicious activities and incidents of fraud to the banking supervisor if such
activities/incidents are material to the safety, soundness or reputation of the bank.
702
If the supervisor becomes aware of any additional suspicious transactions, it informs the
4.
financial intelligence unit and, if applicable, other designated authorities of such
transactions. In addition, the supervisor directly or indirectly shares information related
to suspected or actual criminal activities with relevant authorities, in a timely manner.
The supervisor determines that banks establish CDD policies and processes that are well
5.
documented and communicated to all relevant staﬀ. The supervisor also determines that
such policies and processes are integrated into the bank's overall risk management and
include appropriate steps to identify, assess, monitor, manage and mitigate the risks of
money laundering, terrorist financing and proliferation financing with respect to
customers, countries and regions, as well as to products, services, transactions and
delivery channels on an ongoing basis. The CDD management programme, on a group-
wide basis, has as its essential elements:
a customer acceptance policy that identifies business relationships that the bank
1.
will not accept (or will be terminated) based on identified risks;
an ongoing customer identification, verification and due diligence programme,
2.
which encompasses verification of beneficial ownership, understanding the purpose
and nature of the business relationship, and risk-based reviews to ensure that CDD
information is updated and relevant;
policies and processes to monitor transactions on an ongoing basis and identify
3.
unusual or potentially suspicious transactions as well as those individuals or
entities subject to the United Nations sanctions related to terrorism and
proliferation financing;
enhanced due diligence on high-risk accounts (eg escalation to the bank's senior
4.
management of decisions on entering into business relationships with these
accounts or maintaining such relationships when an existing relationship becomes
high-risk);
enhanced due diligence on politically exposed persons (including their family
5.
members and close associates) encompassing, among other things, escalation to
the bank's senior management of decisions on entering into business relationships
with these persons; and
clear rules on what records must be kept on CDD and individual transactions and
6.
their retention period. Such records have at least a five-year retention period.
The supervisor determines that banks have specific policies and processes regarding
6.
correspondent banking and other similar relationships, in addition to normal due
diligence. Such policies and processes include:
gathering suﬃcient information about their respondent banks to understand fully
1.
the nature of their business and customer base, their reputation, how they are
supervised and whether they have been subject to money laundering, terrorism
financing or proliferation financing investigations or regulatory actions;
prohibitions on establishing or continuing correspondent banking relationships with
2.
those banks that do not have adequate controls to manage the risk of criminal
activities, that are not eﬀectively supervised by the relevant authorities, or that are
considered to be shell banks; and
senior management approval for entering into new correspondent banking
3.
relationships.
The supervisor determines that banks have suﬃcient controls and systems to prevent,
7.
identify and report potential abuses of financial services, including money laundering,
terrorism financing and proliferation financing.
The supervisor has adequate powers to take action against a bank that does not comply
8.
with relevant laws and regulations regarding criminal activities.
The supervisor determines that banks have:
9.
requirements for internal audit and/or external experts to independently evaluate
1.
the relevant risk management policies, processes and controls. The supervisor has
access to their reports;
eﬀective policies and processes to designate a compliance oﬃcer at the bank's
2.
management level to manage the financial crimes compliance programme, and a
dedicated oﬃcer to whom potential abuses of the bank's financial services
(including suspicious transactions) are reported;
a compliance function with adequate powers, reporting independence, staﬀ and
3.
other resources;
adequate screening policies and processes to ensure high ethical and professional
4.
standards when hiring staﬀ or when entering into an agency or outsourcing
relationship;
ongoing training programmes for their staﬀ, including on CDD and methods to
5.
monitor and detect criminal and suspicious activities; and
policies and processes to report criminal activities by staﬀ to competent
6.
authorities.
The supervisor determines that banks have and follow clear policies and processes for
10.
staﬀ to report any issues related to the abuse of the banks' financial services to local
management and/or the relevant dedicated oﬃcer. The supervisor also determines that
banks have and utilise adequate management information systems to provide the banks'
boards, management and dedicated oﬃcers with timely and appropriate information on
such activities.
Laws provide that a member of a bank's staﬀ who reports suspicious activity in good faith
11.
either internally or directly to the relevant authority cannot be held liable.
The supervisor, directly or indirectly, cooperates with relevant domestic and foreign
12.
financial sector authorities or exchanges information with them regarding suspected or
actual criminal activities present in banks, where this information is for supervisory
purposes.
Unless another authority is responsible, the supervisor has in-house resources with
13.
specialist expertise for addressing criminal activities detected in banks. In this case, the
supervisor regularly provides information on the risks of money laundering, terrorism
financing and proliferation financing to the banks.
The supervisor determines that banks have in place group-wide programmes to address
14.
money laundering, terrorist financing and proliferation financing, including policies and
procedures for sharing information within the group for these purposes.
1 Footnote
702
In accordance with international standards, banks are to report suspicious
activities involving cases of potential money laundering, terrorist financing and
proliferation financing to the relevant national centre, which is established
either as an independent governmental authority or as a department within an
existing authority or authorities that serves as a financial intelligence unit.","Basel Framework, BCP40, num. 40.67, pp. 1412-1414","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split+page_split","0.99","false",""
