"c_2439f7521320fd7a","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026","Basel Framework","Basel III - Basel Framework consolidado","BCBS/BIS","INT","international_standard","in_force","","2026-01-01","","https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/basel-framework.pdf","fuentes_descargadas/02_normativa_internacional/18_bis_basel_framework_2026.pdf","acf3759fc231fb8f13131e50b12ad11e18bc9ebe53f105595fd8e16609a3547f","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026#nsec-99-occ8","99","numbered_section","BCP Core Principles for effective banking supervision > 99 Structure and guidance for assessment reports prepared by the International… > num. 99.1-99.3","1418","1419","BCP99
Structure and guidance for
assessment reports prepared
by the International Monetary
Fund and World Bank
This chapter outlines guidance for the
preparation of assessment reports by the
International Monetary Fund and World Bank.
Version eﬀective as of 25
April 2024
First version in the consolidated Basel
Framework.
99.1
This section presents guidance and a format recommended by the IMF and the World Bank for
the presentation and organisation of the Basel Core Principles (BCP) assessment reports by
assessors in the context of the FSAP and standalone assessments. A self-assessment
703
conducted by the country's authorities prior to IMF-World Bank assessments is an essential
element in the process and should also follow this guidance and format.
1 Footnote
703
Such self-assessment should be made available to assessors well in advance –
also considering the possible need for translation – accompanied by the
supporting legislation and regulation.
99.2
The BCP assessment report should be divided into eight parts:
a ""summary and main findings"" section;
1.
a general section providing background information and information on the methodology
2.
used;
an overview of institutional setting and market infrastructure;
3.
a review of preconditions for eﬀective banking supervision;
4.
detailed principle-by-principle assessments;
5.
a compliance table summarising the results of the assessment;
6.
recommended actions; and
7.
the authorities' response.
8.
The following paragraphs provide a brief description of each of the eight parts.
99.3
A short ""summary and main findings section"" should provide an overview of the main findings
and main recommendations of the report. It should read as an executive summary, with the
main findings for several principles aggregated in a few paragraphs under subtitles. For
example, assessors may choose to consolidate findings and recommendations under the
subtitles of Responsibility, objectives, powers, independence and accountability (principles
1–2), Ownership, licensing and structure (principles 4–7), Methods of ongoing supervision
(principles 8–10), Corrective and sanctioning powers of supervisors (principle 11), Cooperation,
consolidated, and cross-border banking supervision (principles 3, 12–13), Corporate
governance (principle 14), Prudential requirements, regulatory framework, accounting and
disclosure (principles 15–29).","Basel Framework, BCP99, num. 99.1-99.3, pp. 1418-1419","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split","0.99","false",""
"c_bf8c4f4b7ac1a7f8","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026","Basel Framework","Basel III - Basel Framework consolidado","BCBS/BIS","INT","international_standard","in_force","","2026-01-01","","https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/basel-framework.pdf","fuentes_descargadas/02_normativa_internacional/18_bis_basel_framework_2026.pdf","acf3759fc231fb8f13131e50b12ad11e18bc9ebe53f105595fd8e16609a3547f","int-bcbs-basel-framework-2026#nsec-99-occ8","99","numbered_section","BCP Core Principles for effective banking supervision > 99 Structure and guidance for assessment reports prepared by the International… > num. 99.4","1419","1420","99.4
A brief introduction and methodology section which provides background information on the
assessment conducted, ie the context in which the assessment is being conducted and the
methodology used. This section should:
Indicate that the scope of the assessment has been selected with the authorities'
1.
agreement, mentioning in particular whether the authorities agreed to be assessed and
graded on the basis of only the essential criteria or on the basis of additional criteria too.
The names and aﬃliations of the assessors should be mentioned in this section.
Mention the sources used for the assessment such as any self-assessments,
2.
questionnaires filled out by the authorities, relevant laws, regulations and instructions,
and other documentation, such as reports, studies, public statements, websites,
unpublished guidelines, directives, supervisory reports and assessments.
Identify counterparty authorities and mention, in a generic way, senior oﬃcials
704 with
3.
whom interviews were held and meetings with other domestic supervisory authorities,
private sector participants, other relevant government authorities or industry
associations (such as bankers' associations, auditors and accountants).
Mention factors that impeded or facilitated the assessment. In particular, information
4.
gaps (such as lack of access to supervisory materials or translated documents) should be
mentioned, and an indication should be given of the extent to which these gaps may
have aﬀected the assessment.
705
2 Footnotes
704
Names are typically avoided, in order to protect individuals and encourage
candour.
705
If the lack of information adversely impacts the quality and depth of the
assessment of a particular Core Principle, assessors should refer to this in the
comment section of the assessment template and document the obstacles
encountered, in particular where access to in-depth information is crucial in
evaluating compliance. Such issues should be brought to the attention of the
mission leaders and, where necessary, referred to headquarters staﬀ for
guidance.","Basel Framework, BCP99, num. 99.4, pp. 1419-1420","pymupdf+bookmark_title_match+numeral_split","0.99","false",""
